        "title": "Tree Anatomy: Vascular Cambium, Sapwood, Heartwood, Bark",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Outermost\n  |\n  |  [1] RHYTIDOME (outer bark)\n  |      Layers of dead periderm (cork) + compressed dead phloem\n  |      Moisture barrier; insulation; pest/fire resistance\n  |\n  |  [2] INNER BARK (functional phloem)\n  |      Sieve tubes + companion cells: sugar transport downward\n  |      Phloem parenchyma stores starch; short-lived (1–3 yr active)\n  |\n  |  [3] VASCULAR CAMBIUM  <--- PRIMARY BOUNDARY\n  |      One-cell-thick meristematic cylinder\n  |      Fusiform initials --> xylem (inward) + phloem (outward)\n  |      Ray initials --> wood rays (radial parenchyma)\n  |\n  |  [4] SAPWOOD (functional xylem: outermost N rings)\n  |      Water + mineral transport (xylem vessels or tracheids)\n  |      Living ray parenchyma: starch storage, heartwood precursors\n  |      Xylem fibers: mechanical support\n  |\n  |  [5] HEARTWOOD  <--- TRANSITION ZONE\n  |      Dead xylem saturated with extractive compounds\n  |      Tylose formation in vessels (blocks conductivity deliberately)\n  |      Color from phenolics, tannins, oils, silica\n  |\n  |  [6] PITH\n  |      Dead parenchyma at geometric center\n  |      First-year's primary growth; often hollow in older stems\n  |\n  Innermost\n```"
